      "id": 1,
      "text": "The village was renamed Thebes in 1843, echoing the ancient Egyptian capital, a nod to local rock formations that resembled Nile Valley scenery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/thebes_illinois",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Thebes served as Alexander County's seat from 1846 until 1859 before the capital moved to Cairo.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thebes%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Abraham Lincoln argued cases in Thebes' 1848 Greek Revival courthouse, which later housed Dred Scott in its basement jail.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/thebes_illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Thebes Railroad Bridge, a cantilever marvel opened in 1905, still carries trains across the Mississippi.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/thebes_illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Thebes' Mississippi River depth exceeds four feet, once making it a bustling steamboat hub.",
